  "frontMatter": {
    "abstract": [
      {
        "text": "The success of deep learning in infrared small target detection (IRSTD) relies on large-scale, precise pixel-level annotations, yet their high acquisition cost and inevitable noise impede further progress. Recently, point-supervised learning has demonstrated remarkable potential in mitigating these issues in IRSTD. The dominant paradigm involves generating pixel level pseudo-labels from point annotations, then applied to train deep segmentation networks. However, some proposed pseudo label generation strategies rely on measuring intensity similarity and spatial proximity between pixels to classify them. This approach leads to an unreasonable target probability distribution wherein, even among pixels that truly belong to the target, those close to the certain point, typically annotated point, are assigned high probabilities, while those farther away receive significantly lower probabilities. To address this, we propose a shape-prior-driven framework—Gradient-Guided Minimum Variance Growth Filter with Deep Learning Refinement—that produces high-quality pseudo-labels by modeling edge intensity and closure, followed by iterative refinement with a deep seg mentation network. Leveraging structural consistency over pixel level similarity, our approach effectively handles irregular and low-saliency targets. Crucially, our method avoids distance-based assumptions, yielding robustness to point annotation deviations. The proposed method generally outperforms existing approaches as validated by plenty of experiments on three representative datasets.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
